<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Love song · 1914 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Madelon</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fr">Quand Madelon...</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A cheerful French song of the First World War about soldiers joking with a young waitress at a country inn.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Louis Bousquet</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Camille Robert</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French soldiers (poilus) of the First World War</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Paris, France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 19 March 1914; first recorded 1917</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Louis Bousquet (d. 1941), first performed 1914; music by Camille Robert (d. 1957))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Quand Madelon...&quot;, usually called &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-madelon">La Madelon</a>&quot;, has words by Louis Bousquet (1870–1941) and music by Camille Robert (1872–1957). In 1913 the singer Bach (Charles-Joseph Pasquier) asked them for a new patriotic song; the music began as a march for a brass band, to which Bousquet wrote cheerful words. Bach first sang it on 19 March 1914 at the café-concert l'Eldorado in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where it had little success.</p><p>The war made it famous. In August 1914 the songwriter Sioul, who had heard the first performance and was now an artillery soldier billeted in the Jules-Ferry school at Fontenay-sous-Bois, sang it to his comrades. Bach sang it to soldiers on leave in the army <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and on 16 June 1916 he sang it at the inn of mother Misquette at Étival-Clairefontaine behind the front in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, where it was a great success; from there it spread along the front, carried by the gunners and by the comic soldier-singers popular in the war. Its words were clean at a time when most soldiers' songs were bawdy, which may partly explain its long popularity, and as the war went on it became a patriotic song. Marcelly made the first recording in 1917; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the Victor Military Band recorded it in 1919 with the French singer Marcel Journet, and Arthur Fields sang an English version in 1918. It also became popular among Spanish soldiers. In 1921 plaques were placed on the school at Fontenay-sous-Bois and at Étival-Clairefontaine to mark the song's two first successes at the front.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> soldiers sang it in thanks to Clotilde Bizolon (1871–1940), &quot;the mother of the poilus&quot;, who kept a refreshment stall for soldiers at Perrache station and was given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour in 1925; she became the city's own Madelon. Lucien Boyer wrote a sequel, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-madelon">La Madelon</a> de la Victoire&quot;, in 1919.</p><p>The song was revived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>: Marlene Dietrich sang it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> at the celebration of 14 July 1939. Films drew on it, from Madelon's Daughter (1937) to Jean Boyer's comedy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-madelon">La Madelon</a> (1955), in which Line Renaud sang it surrounded by soldiers, and the Spanish singer Sara Montiel sang it in El Último Cuplé (1957). Louis-Ferdinand Céline questioned the easy reputation of the song's Madelon in Voyage au bout de la nuit (1932). It remains a well-known patriotic song in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, though the scholar François Genton holds that its words and tune now interest people mainly as historical documents.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>Soldiers flirt with a lovely young waitress in a country tavern. Light, cheerful and clean, it was sung as a soldiers' song and became a patriotic one.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour le repos, le plaisir du militaire,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the rest and the pleasure of the soldier,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il est là-bas à deux pas de la forêt</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There is, over there, two steps from the forest,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Une maison aux murs tout couverts de lierre</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A house with walls all covered in ivy;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">« Aux Tourlourous » c'est le nom du cabaret.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Aux Tourlourous” is the name of the tavern.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La servante est jeune et gentille</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The serving girl is young and nice,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Légère comme un papillon.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Light as a butterfly.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Comme son vin son œil pétille,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Like her wine, her eye sparkles;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous l'appelons la Madelon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We call her the Madelon.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous en rêvons la nuit, nous y pensons le jour,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We dream of it at night, we think of it by day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ce n'est que Madelon mais pour nous c'est l'amour</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is only Madelon, but for us it is love.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Refrain :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Refrain:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand Madelon vient nous servir à boire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When Madelon comes to pour us something to drink,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sous la tonnelle on frôle son jupon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Under the arbour we brush against her petticoat,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et chacun lui raconte une histoire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And each of us tells her a story,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Une histoire à sa façon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A story in his own way.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La Madelon pour nous n'est pas sévère</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Madelon is not strict with us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand on lui prend la taille ou le menton</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When we take her by the waist or the chin;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Elle rit, c'est tout le mal qu'elle sait faire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She laughs, that is all the harm she knows how to do.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Madelon, Madelon, Madelon !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Madelon, Madelon, Madelon!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous avons tous au pays une payse</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We all have back home a girl from our parts</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Qui nous attend et que l'on épousera</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who is waiting for us and whom we will marry,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais elle est loin, bien trop loin pour qu'on lui dise</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But she is far away, much too far for us to tell her</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ce qu'on fera quand la classe rentrera</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What we will do when our class goes home.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">En comptant les jours on soupire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Counting the days, we sigh,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et quand le temps nous semble long</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And when the time seems long to us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tout ce qu'on ne peut pas lui dire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All that we cannot tell her</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On va le dire à Madelon</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We go and tell Madelon.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On l'embrasse dans les coins. Elle dit « veux-tu finir… »</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We kiss her in the corners. She says, “Will you stop…”</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On s'figure que c'est l'autre, ça nous fait bien plaisir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We imagine she is the other one; that pleases us a lot.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Un caporal en képi de fantaisie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A corporal in a fancy kepi</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">S'en fut trouver Madelon un beau matin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Went to find Madelon one fine morning</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et, fou d'amour, lui dit qu'elle était jolie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And, mad with love, told her she was pretty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et qu'il venait pour lui demander sa main</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And that he had come to ask for her hand.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La Madelon, pas bête, en somme,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Madelon, no fool after all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Lui répondit en souriant :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Answered him with a smile:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et pourquoi prendrais-je un seul homme</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And why would I take one man alone</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand j'aime tout un régiment ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When I love a whole regiment?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tes amis vont venir. Tu n'auras pas ma main</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your friends will come. You will not have my hand;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J'en ai bien trop besoin pour leur verser du vin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I need it far too much to pour them wine.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Louis Bousquet (1914). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quand_Madelon..." target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>line 1.4: 'Tourlourou' is old soldiers' slang for an infantryman; the tavern's name means 'At the Foot Soldiers''. Kept as a name.</li><li>line 1.8: 'la Madelon' is a familiar form of Madeleine; the article before a woman's name is colloquial.</li><li>line 2.1: 'Refrain :' is a label and is kept.</li><li>line 3.1: 'une payse' is colloquial for a girl from one's own village or district, a sweetheart back home.</li><li>line 3.4: 'quand la classe rentrera' = when the class goes home: 'la classe' is the year's intake of conscripts, sent home together at the end of their service.</li><li>line 3.9: 'veux-tu finir…' = will you stop (that)…, a mild protest.</li><li>line 3.10: 'On s'figure' is spoken French for 'on se figure', we imagine; 'l'autre' is the sweetheart at home.</li><li>line 4.1: 'képi de fantaisie' = a kepi not of the regulation pattern, a smart one bought privately.</li><li>line 4.10: 'main' plays on the hand asked in marriage (4.4) and the hand that pours the wine.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
